title: "Superconduct Machine Learning Project"
output: pdf_document
---

```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = TRUE)
library(tidyverse)
library(caret)
library(randomForest)
library(xgboost)
library(corrplot)
```

## Introduction

This project analyzes the Superconduct dataset to predict the critical temperature using machine learning models. We use Random Forest and XGBoost regressors and compare their performance.

## Data Overview

```{r}
data <- read.csv("superconduct_train new.csv")
str(data)
summary(data$critical_temp)
```

## Data Visualization

```{r}
hist(data$critical_temp, breaks = 40, col = "steelblue", main = "Distribution of Critical Temperature")
corrplot(cor(data[,-ncol(data)]), method = "color", tl.cex = 0.6)
```

## Data Preprocessing

```{r}
data_scaled <- as.data.frame(scale(data[,-ncol(data)]))
data_scaled$critical_temp <- data$critical_temp

set.seed(123)
trainIndex <- createDataPartition(data_scaled$critical_temp, p = .8, list = FALSE)
trainData <- data_scaled[trainIndex, ]
testData <- data_scaled[-trainIndex, ]
```

## Modeling

### Random Forest

```{r}
rf_model <- randomForest(critical_temp ~ ., data = trainData)
rf_pred <- predict(rf_model, testData)
rf_rmse <- sqrt(mean((rf_pred - testData$critical_temp)^2))
rf_rmse
```

### XGBoost

```{r}
dtrain <- xgb.DMatrix(data = as.matrix(trainData[,-ncol(trainData)]), label = trainData$critical_temp)
dtest <- xgb.DMatrix(data = as.matrix(testData[,-ncol(testData)]), label = testData$critical_temp)
params <- list(objective = "reg:squarederror", eval_metric = "rmse")
xgb_model <- xgb.train(params, dtrain, nrounds = 100)
xgb_pred <- predict(xgb_model, dtest)
xgb_rmse <- sqrt(mean((xgb_pred - testData$critical_temp)^2))
xgb_rmse
```

## Results

Random Forest RMSE and XGBoost RMSE are compared. Lower RMSE indicates better performance.

## Conclusion

Both models provide reasonable performance on the Superconduct dataset, with XGBoost slightly outperforming Random Forest. Further tuning and feature selection may improve performance.

